        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The squeegee spreads fastest and controls coat thickness best, which matters because too thick is what makes sealer stay tacky and peel. The brush works material into surface texture, which is where adhesion comes from on rough or porous asphalt. The roller belongs on smooth concrete and small areas, and is the weakest choice on a textured drive.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Which is why the professional tool has both: pull the sealer out with the blade to spread it evenly, then work back over it with the bristles to drive it in and even out the streaks. That combination is the method rather than a compromise, and it suits almost every asphalt driveway.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever you use, the shared technique matters more than the choice. Apply at the label coverage rate, two thin coats rather than one heavy one, keep a wet edge and work in sections, cut the edges first, and clean the tools immediately while the sealer is still wet.
          </p>
